## 00910 PATIENT=1 DOCTOR=2
1: Doctor, I'm still getting this pressure in my face. The spray you gave me isn't doing much. ~ cc s1
2: Thanks for coming back. Where do you feel the pressure? ~ s1
1: Around my cheeks and across my forehead. It's worse when I bend over. ~ s1
2: And how long has it been going on now? ~ s1
1: Nearly three weeks. ~ s1
2: Is there discharge from the nose, and what colour? ~ s2
1: Thick and yellowish, mostly in the morning. ~ s2
2: Any fever, or swelling around the eyes? ~ n1
1: No fever. And my eyes look normal. ~ n1
2: Let's go through what you're taking. Which spray is it? ~ m1
1: The steroid nasal spray, fluticasone, two sprays in each nostril, once a day. ~ m1
2: Are you using it every day? ~ m1 u1
1: Most days. I forget some mornings, to be honest. ~ m1 u1
2: Anything else? ~ m2
1: Paracetamol, 1 gram, when the face is sore. And a saline rinse, twice a day. ~ m2 m3
2: Good, the rinse helps. Do you have any other conditions, like hay fever or asthma? ~ h1
1: Hay fever, every summer. ~ h1
2: Let me check your pulse, blood pressure and temperature. ~
1: Okay. ~ pause=10
2: Pulse 74, blood pressure 124 over 80, temperature 36.8, oxygen 99 percent, breathing 14. ~ v
1: All normal? ~
2: All normal. I'd like to look in your nose and press over the sinuses. ~
1: Go on. ~ pause=14
2: I would say this is sinusitis, probably still viral, though the thick discharge makes me think of a bacterial cause. ~ d1
1: Do I need antibiotics? ~
2: Not yet. Three weeks is long enough that I'd consider it, but let's do the basics properly first. ~
1: What are the basics? ~
2: Use the spray every single day, keep up the saline rinse, and take steam inhalations at night. ~ c1
1: I will. ~
2: If it hasn't improved in a week, or you get a fever or swelling around the eyes, I'll prescribe an antibiotic. ~ f1
1: That sounds fair. ~
2: Come back and see me then, and we'll decide together. ~
1: Thank you, doctor. ~
@@
